    {
      "id": "p1",
      "text": "As spring weather offered a seasonal window of opportunity, the state's immigration enforcement arm reported a spike in border interceptions along the southwest border. Over one month, apprehensions surged, a statistical trend that observers of state power attribute to a harsh crackdown. Yet these numbers belie the larger picture: historically low crossing numbers tied to restrictive policies have created dramatically reduced movement under punitive rules. The political deadlock continues, with politicians entangled in legislative stalemate failing to secure the funding needed to maintain this machinery of control.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Outlaw",
        "fragments": [
          "why-0",
          "how-0",
          "result-0",
          "result-1"
        ],
        "channel_role": "toned"
      },
      "warnings": [],
      "fit": 0.85
    },
    {
      "id": "p2",
      "text": "Federal law enforcement along the U.S. border saw apprehensions increase, a numerical change that masks the true cost. While lawmakers remain deadlocked—a legislative impasse on enforcement funding—those seeking entry become people caught by the system. This enforcement policy outcome, framed as a border security effect, is really about protecting entrenched power. The funding dispute is not a failure of governance but its success: political gridlock protects the machinery of control, ensuring the status quo of suppression continues unchallenged.",
      "source": {
        "agent_id": "The-Outlaw",
